{
	title: 'SYSTEM.md: the Hal prompt lives in a file',
	spec: "Hal's own system prompt text lives in SYSTEM.md at the root of the Hal checkout, not in code: the one-line identity becomes the start of that file. systemPrompt.build reads it as one of its inputs (like the AGENTS.md chain), so the prompt stays byte-identical between requests while the file is unchanged, and an edited SYSTEM.md takes effect on the next request without a restart. The file starts minimal: who Hal is, and a few rules from the old one (tasks/29/old-SYSTEM.md) that hold for Hal today (tools it really has; no eval, spawn or IPC text until those exist). It grows by the user's edits, not by agents inventing policy. The OAuth identity-block rules of task 29 still hold (blank line before our block).",
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		"systemPrompt.file() is the checkout's SYSTEM.md (resolved from import.meta.dir, not HAL_HOME, so test homes still get it); build reads it per call, trimmed, as the first part before <date>/<cwd>/<model>. A missing file throws with its path (a broken checkout), no fallback text. Tests swap systemPrompt.file to a temp file.",
		"The initial SYSTEM.md keeps only old rules true for Hal when written: git checkout/restore/stash, paths, /cd is the user's command (the model has no eval to run it), 25 lines, the lazy ladder, `read` over bash, <meta> markup. Dropped: eval, spawn, IPC, teleport, grep/glob/edit, <synthetic>, the self-improvement rule and template variables/conditionals. Add rules back as those tools land (task gr adds tools), only on the user's say."
	],
	needs: ['29'],
	foldInto: ['29']
}
